>>
>> In short: don't blow up your bag next time.
> 
> And yet so very few of those unforgiving aspects make the game
> literally unwinnable except but not lethal. It could be said that an
> unwinnable game should NEVER be a possibility.

Let us, rather, say that an unwinnable game should never be the result
of pure random chance.  (This is why the amulet and invocation items are
indestructible; it is the simplest method to cover _all_ the holes by
which items might be splashed.  And yet you still mention one that was
missed; bribing a demon who has the Amulet, though that takes a great
deal of work to accomplish.)

> some would say that the pre-quest
> conversion sequence is similarly a flaw in game design.

I'm not particularly enthused about that, no, because the rationale for
being unable to convert back is both logical and puzzling at the same
time.  It should certainly be significantly difficult to convert back,
but not directly impossible.

> Loss of valuable equipment - which, by the way, could occur not just
> by typos but by a nymph stealing the player's bag and then being
> disintegrated by black dragon friendly fire - should not make a game
> literally unwinnable.

And it won't make the game unwinnable, either.  The invocation items
will be available; if the candles were on the Candelabrum, they'll still
be there.

It may make the game extremely difficult, for sure, but if the player
needs to resort to extinctionism, that is still available to him.  As
far as the case of the player who extincted all extinctable monsters and
still doesn't have enough candles, I'm going to wait for an actual
report of this actually happening before I worry about it happening.

